re</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Trace eBGP underlay, EVPN overlay and tenant routing separately.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-sample-configurations">https://www.arista.com/en/um-eos/eos-sample-configurations</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b><a href="https://www.arista.com/assets/data/images/EVPN%20L3%20Basic%20Diagram.jpg">https://www.arista.com/assets/data/images/EVPN%20L3%20Basic%20Diagram.jpg</a></p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Aruba/HPE</span><h3>VSX logical-VTEP figure reference</h3><div class="fb" style="display:block"><a href="https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf">Open source technical figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Separate BGP speaker identities from the shared logical VTEP.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf">https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.15/PDF/vsx.pdf</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b>No stable raster URL exposed by the source; use the source-article link above to view the original figure.</p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">Juniper</span><h3>IPv6-underlay EVPN figures</h3><div class="fb" style="display:block"><a href="https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/topic-map/vxlan-ipv6-underlay-overview.html">Open source technical figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Outer IPv6 transport is separate from EVPN route semantics.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/topic-map/vxlan-ipv6-underlay-overview.html">https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/evpn/topics/topic-map/vxlan-ipv6-underlay-overview.html</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b>No stable raster URL exposed by the source; use the source-article link above to view the original figure.</p></div></div><div class="fig"><span class="badge">FRRouting</span><h3>FRR EVPN Linux model</h3><div class="fb" style="display:block"><a href="https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html">Open source technical figure</a></div><p><b>What to notice:</b> Kernel bridge/SVI/VRF objects are part of forwarding correctness.</p><div class="source-links"><p><b>Source article: </b><a href="https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html">https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html</a></p><p><b>Original image URL: </b>No stable raster URL exposed by the source; use the source-article link above to view the original figure.</p></div></div></div><h2>Multivendor comparison</h2><section class="card"><table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Portable rule</th></tr><tr><td>Redistribution</td><td>Filter narrowly, preserve provenance with tags/communities, deny re-entry, and know whether only active RIB routes qualify.</td></tr><tr><td>EVPN/VXLAN</td><td>Underlay VTEP reachability first; then EVPN import, VNI/VRF mappings, multichassis state, FIB and packet test.</td></tr><tr><td>Policy translation</td><td>Translate intent and attachment stage before syntax.</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Reachability and control-plane correctness do not prove hardware offload, kernel resource health or efficient BUM replication.</td></tr></table></section><h2>Evidence-first troubleshooting playbook</h2><section class="card"><ol><li>Physical/L2, LACP/MLAG/VSX/vPC, VLAN and MTU.</li><li>OSPF/EIGRP/IS-IS/BGP/BFD adjacency.</li><li>Source protocol route and path type.</li><li>Redistribution/import/export policy.</li><li>Winning RIB route and next-hop recursion.</li><li>FIB/hardware/kernel programming.</li><li>EVPN VNI/VRF/ESI/RT and BUM state.</li><li>Real packet/SLA/application test.</li></ol></section><h2>50-question cumulative exam</h2><section class="card"><div class="controls"><button onclick="grade()">Submit / Grade</button><button onclick="reset()">Reset</button></div><div id="score"></div></section><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>1. FortiGate SD-WAN sees two Established eBGP peers but only one RIB next hop. Best next step?</h3><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Make both BGP paths eligible/installed with multipath</label><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Raise OSPF priority</label><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Change VNI</label><label><input name="q1" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Enable STP root guard</label><button onclick="cq(1)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f1"></div><span id="r1" style="display:none">Correct answer: Make both BGP paths eligible/installed with multipath. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>2. Why preserve the EVPN VTEP next hop through a Cisco spine?</h3><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> The spine must decapsulate VXLAN</label><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Remote leaves must resolve and tunnel directly to the actual VTEP</label><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> To disable recursion</label><label><input name="q2" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> To replace BGP</label><button onclick="cq(2)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f2"></div><span id="r2" style="display:none">Correct answer: Remote leaves must resolve and tunnel directly to the actual VTEP. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>3. What does Aruba VSX logical-VTEP design demonstrate?</h3><label><input name="q3" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Both BGP speakers share one router ID</label><label><input name="q3" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VSX has one CPU</label><label><input name="q3" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Control-plane identity and forwarding identity can differ</label><label><input name="q3" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> The underlay is optional</label><button onclick="cq(3)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f3"></div><span id="r3" style="display:none">Correct answer: Control-plane identity and forwarding identity can differ. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>4. What changes with a Junos IPv6 underlay?</h3><label><input name="q4" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> EVPN route types disappear</label><label><input name="q4" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VXLAN uses TCP</label><label><input name="q4" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP is unsupported</label><label><input name="q4" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Outer VXLAN/VTEP reachability uses IPv6 while EVPN remains the overlay control plane</label><button onclick="cq(4)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f4"></div><span id="r4" style="display:none">Correct answer: Outer VXLAN/VTEP reachability uses IPv6 while EVPN remains the overlay control plane. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>5. Why can FRR EVPN routes be correct while forwarding fails?</h3><label><input name="q5" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Linux bridge/SVI/VRF/kernel state can be wrong</label><label><input name="q5" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> FRR never programs Linux</label><label><input name="q5" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> EVPN has no data plane</label><label><input name="q5" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> BGP requires RIP</label><button onclick="cq(5)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f5"></div><span id="r5" style="display:none">Correct answer: Linux bridge/SVI/VRF/kernel state can be wrong. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>6. Why can an Arista single-gateway fabric duplicate BUM?</h3><label><input name="q6" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> BGP sends data twice</label><label><input name="q6" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Redundant VTEP identities can both enter the floodset</label><label><input name="q6" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> VXLAN has no split horizon</label><label><input name="q6" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> OSPF duplicates MACs</label><button onclick="cq(6)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f6"></div><span id="r6" style="display:none">Correct answer: Redundant VTEP identities can both enter the floodset. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>7. PAN-OS route present in local BGP RIB but absent from one peer: inspect what?</h3><label><input name="q7" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Redistribution admission</label><label><input name="q7" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> OSPF DR priority</label><label><input name="q7" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Peer/group export policy</label><label><input name="q7" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> MTU only</label><button onclick="cq(7)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f7"></div><span id="r7" style="display:none">Correct answer: Peer/group export policy. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>8. FRR redistributed external inside an NSSA becomes what?</h3><label><input name="q8" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> EVPN Type-5</label><label><input name="q8" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Dropped always</label><label><input name="q8" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> iBGP</label><label><input name="q8" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Type-7 LSA</label><button onclick="cq(8)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f8"></div><span id="r8" style="display:none">Correct answer: Type-7 LSA. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>9. AOS-CX active-routes-only means what?</h3><label><input name="q9" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> Only routes selected for forwarding are redistribution candidates</label><label><input name="q9" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> All protocol entries redistribute</label><label><input name="q9" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Only statics redistribute</label><label><input name="q9" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> Route maps are disabled</label><button onclick="cq(9)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f9"></div><span id="r9" style="display:none">Correct answer: Only routes selected for forwarding are redistribution candidates. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>10. FortiOS high route-tag caveat can break what design?</h3><label><input name="q10" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> OSPF adjacency</label><label><input name="q10" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> Tag-based route provenance/loop prevention</label><label><input name="q10" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> BGP TCP authentication</label><label><input name="q10" type="radio" value="3"/> <b>D.</b> VXLAN UDP port</label><button onclick="cq(10)">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="f10"></div><span id="r10" style="display:none">Correct answer: Tag-based route provenance/loop prevention. The distractors act at a different protocol layer or policy stage.</span></div><div class="q"><span class="badge">Current</span><h3>11. Why must Cisco all-active EVPN peers have consistent service state?</h3><label><input name="q11" type="radio" value="0"/> <b>A.</b> BGP requires same hostname</label><label><input name="q11" type="radio" value="1"/> <b>B.</b> VTEPs share management IP</label><label><input name="q11" type="radio" value="2"/> <b>C.</b> Inconsistency can black-hole traffic 